```cpp
template<typename T>
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) {
    { a + b } -> std::convertible_to<T>;
};
```
This defines `Addable` as true if `a + b` is a valid expression and its result is convertible to `T`.

```cpp
template<typename T>
concept Addable = requires(T a, T b) {
    { a + b } -> std::convertible_to<T>;
};

template<typename T>
T add_two_values(T a, T b) requires Addable<T> {
    return a + b;
}
```

1.  **Concept as Type Parameter:**
    ```cpp
    template<Addable T>
    T add_two_values(T a, T b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    ```
2.  **Abbreviated Function Template:**
    ```cpp
    Addable auto add_two_values(Addable auto a, Addable auto b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    ```
    Note: For the abbreviated form, `Addable auto` implies `Addable<decltype(a)>` and `Addable<decltype(b)>`.

```cpp
#include <type_traits> // For std::is_arithmetic_v
#include <concepts>    // For std::integral, std::floating_point

template<typename T>
concept Arithmetic = (std::integral<T> || std::floating_point<T>) && std::is_arithmetic_v<T>;
```
This demonstrates combining concepts using logical operators (`||` for OR, `&&` for AND).
